$\gamma$ -Cyclodextrin-based metal-organic framework $\mathsf{\Omega}\cdot\mathsf{V C D-M O F},$ ) crystals were successfully synthesized using a vapor diffusion method. An applicability of ${\tt\gamma}_{\mathrm{{VCD-MOF}}}$ for encapsulation of immunosuppressive disease-modifying antirheumatic drug leflunomide (LEF) was examined. Loading of LEF in $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF was performed by impregnation and co-crystallization. The empty and loaded $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOFs were characterized using X-ray powder diffraction, $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption, thermogravimetric analysis, $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR and FTIR spectroscopy. It was shown that in the presence of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ leflunomide is transformed into its pharmacologically active form – teriflunomide that can be also applied alone in the treatment of multiple sclerosis. It was demonstrated that teriflunomide released from $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF has improved pharmacologically relevant properties such as solubility, dissolution rate and membrane permeability. It can be proposed that $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF can be considered as novel strategy for delivery of leflunomide.
## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) represent a perspective class of hybrid porous compounds having numerous applications in science and industry (Forgan et al., 2012; Prochowicz, Kornowicz, & Lewiński, 2017). They are efficient for gas adsorption and storage, chemical sensing, gas, catalysis, separation and drug (Chen et al., 2009; Huang, Liang, Wang, & Cao, 2017; Rieter, Taylor, & Lin, 2007; Seo et al., 2000). Biomedical application of MOFs received special attention in recent years due to biocompatibility and a high loading capacity of these materials. In this connection, MOFs are considered to be promising platforms for drug delivery (Wu & Yang, 2017).
Recently, cyclodextrin-based biologically relevant MOFs were discovered. The cyclodextrin-based MOFs (CD-MOFs) can be easily prepared upon crystallization of cyclodextrins (CDs) with cations. CDMOFs have a hierarchic structure with high specific surface area facilitating the ability to encapsulate the drug not only in the CD cavities but also in the pores. In this connection, CD-MOFs are becoming more popular drug delivery systems compared with the parent CDs. There are some works devoted to drug encapsulation in CD-MOFs (Abuçafy et al., 2018; Hartlieb et al., 2017; Li et al., 2017; Liu et al., 2017). Abuçafy et al. (2018) employed MOF composed of $\upgamma$ -CD coordinated with cations $(\mathrm{K}^{+},\mathrm{Na}^{+},\mathrm{Fe}^{3+})$ for delivery of anti-inflammatory drug sodium diclofenac. Inflammation experiments performed in mice demonstrated the applicability of these materials on the design of controlled release systems or in colon-specific drug delivery systems with a greater emphasis on $\upgamma$ -FeCD (Abuçafy et al., 2018). Moussa, Hmadeh, Abiad, Dib, and Patra (2016) encapsulated curcumin in CD-MOFs. It has been detected that stability of curcumin in CD-MOF was enhanced by at least 3 orders of magnitude as compared to free curcumin and curcumin: $\gamma$ -CD. These results suggest that CD-MOFs can be used to store and stabilize curcumin for food applications (Moussa et al., 2016). The ibuprofen/ CD-MOF pharmaceutical cocrystal was designed by Hartlieb et al. (2017). The bioavailability investigations were conducted on mice and ibuprofen/CD-MOF was compared with control samples of the potassium salt of ibuprofen in the presence and absence of $\gamma$ -CD. From these animal studies, it was observed that the ibuprofen/CD-MOF cocrystal exhibits the same rapid uptake of ibuprofen as the ibuprofen potassium salt control sample with a peak plasma concentration observed within $20\mathrm{min}$ ., while the CD-MOF based cocrystal has a benefit of a $100\%$ longer half-life in blood plasma samples and it is intrinsically less hygroscopic than the pure salt form (Hartlieb et al., 2017). It was emphasized by Li and Huang (2008) that the pharmacokinetic properties of the drugs encapsulated in MOF can be improved. To this end, it seems perspective to develop MOF-based delivery systems for poor soluble drug compounds in order to increase their solubility and
bioavailability.
Leflunomide (LEF, Fig. 1) is a synthetic isoxazole derivative which is used clinically as immunomodulator and disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drug. It is approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis since 1998 (Fox & Helfgott, 2004). LEF is a prodrug, which undergoes a conversion to its active metabolite teriflunomide (TEF, Fig. 1). Transformation of LEF to TEF takes place in vivo under enzymes action (Kalgutkar, 2003). This process can be also initiated in vitro by hydroxyl ions acting as catalysts (Sharma, Gangopadhyay, Mishra, Mishra, & Singh, 2016). Charge transfer followed by proton transfer was observed in the reaction solution at basic pH (Sharma et al., 2016). As it has been demonstrated by Sharma et al. (2016), the therapeutic action of LEF as a drug starts after formation of its metabolite, and the metabolic reaction should be considered during the design of the LEF dosage forms.
It has been also shown that LEF is not only the anti-rheumatoid agent, it also displays a good antitumor potential (Zhang & Chu, 2018) and can be used in the therapy in multiple myeloma (Baumann, MandlWeber, Volkl, Adam, & Bumeder, 2009) and neuroblastoma (Kujawski, Bernard, Jodłowska, Czaja, & Drabińska, 2015). In addition, teriflunomide was approved in 2012 as an oral medication for multiple sclerosis (Asheesh & Parul, 2016; Miller, 2017; Mulakayala, Rao, Iqbal, Bandichhor, & Oruganti, 2013).
The LEF and TEF display undesirable side effects including hepatotoxicity and gastrointestinal irritation. Moreover, LEF and TEF are slightly soluble in aqueous solutions (Bae & Park, 2015). According to Biopharmaceutical Classification System, TEF belongs to class II compounds (Asheesh & Parul, 2016). These factors complicate the oral administration of LEF and TEF. To address these limitations, new drug delivery systems (Siafaka, Barmbalexis, & Bikiaris, 2016) and transdermal formulations are proposed (Xi et al., 2013; Zhang, Cun, Kong, & Fang, 2014). Namely, novel electrospun nanofibrous matrices prepared from poly(lacticacid)/poly(butylene adipate) blends for controlled release formulations of an anti-rheumatoid agent were designed (Siafaka et al., 2016). Pandey et al. (2018) designed the TEF loaded hydroxyapatite nanoparticles displaying the maximizing therapeutic efficacy and minimizing side effects. It was also demonstrated in our previous work (Terekhova, Kuranov, Volkova, Chibunova, & Kumeev, 2016) that solubility of LEF can be enhanced via inclusion complex formation with CDs. From this point of view, CD-MOFs can be considered as alternative drug delivery systems for LEF.
In this work, we used $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ that can be easily synthesized from $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and KOH by vapor diffusion method. The synthesis procedure and structure of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ have been well described in the literature (Smaldone et al., 2010). The LEF was loaded in γCD-MOF by impregnation and co-crystallization. The obtained compositions were characterized by different experimental techniques. The influence of the loading method on the entrapment efficiency was revealed. The possibility of transformation of LEF to its pharmacologically active metabolite TEF in $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ was considered. The pharmacologically important properties (solubility, dissolution rate and membrane permeability) of LEF loaded in $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF were investigated in vitro. The effects of ${\tt\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt\tt{\tt{\tt}{\tt\tt{\tt{\tt\tt{\tt}{\tt\tt{\tt}{\tt\tt{\tt}{\tt\tt{\tt}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}$ and native $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ on LEF properties were compared.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Leflunomide (N-[4-(trifluoro-methyl)phenyl]-5-methylisoxazol-4- carboxamide), $\upgamma$ -cyclodextrin $\left(\gamma\mathrm{-CD}\right)$ and potassium hydroxide were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich and used without future purification. All other chemicals (solvents and buffer components) were of analytical grade.
## 2.2. Preparation of the samples
$\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF was prepared according to the procedure well described in the literature (Smaldone et al., 2010). Briefly, $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and KOH were dissolved in water in 1:8 molar ratio. The aqueous solution was then filtered and stored under methanol vapor during 6 days. After that the colorless crystals were isolated, washed with methanol to remove the unbonded potassium ions and dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight under vacuum. Product yield was $74\%$ .
Impregnation and co-crystallyzation methods were used to load LEF in the activated $\mathsf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{-MOF}$ . In case of impregnation, the above-prepared ${\tt\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt{\tt\tt{\tt{\tt}{\tt\tt{\tt{\tt\tt{\tt}{\tt\tt{\tt{\tt\tt{\tt}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}}$ powders were placed in the vials filled with ethanol solution of LEF $(0.018\mathbf{M})$ and were shaken at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ during 2 days. Then the powder of loaded $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ was separated from solution by filtration, washed by EtOH ( $\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}_{6\mathrm{mL}})$ to remove the drug adsorbed on the outer surface of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ , and dried at ambient conditions.
The co-crystallization protocol is similar to that described for synthesis of parent $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF. Crystallization proceeded from the aqueous solution containing $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ , KOH and LEF $(0.18\mathrm{M})$ . Product yield was $65\%$ .
The $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{CD-MOFs}$ loaded by impregnation and co-crystallization methods were named as $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2, respectively.
Drug content in the loaded $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOFs was determined using UV–vis spectroscopy (Shimadzu UV-1800). For this purpose, the precisely weighted samples of $\mathrm{\gamma{\mathsf{CD}}{\cdot}{M O F}{-1}}$ and $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{CD-MOF-}2$ were dissolved in phosphate buffer $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ . The drug content was calculated through UV–vis spectroscopy data $(\lambda=291\mathrm{{nm}}$ ). The weight percentage $(w)$ of the drug in $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF was calculated as follows:
$$
w=\frac{g(d r u g)}{g(d r u g/M O F)}\cdot100\%
$$
where $g(d r u g)$ and $g(d r u g/M O F)$ are weights of drug and $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF loaded by drug, respectively.
Additionally, the encapsulation efficiency (EE), which represents the percentage of drug that is successfully entrapped into $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ was calculated:
$$
E E=\frac{g(d r u g)}{g_{0}(d r u g)}{\cdot}100\%\
$$
where $g_{0}(d r u g)$ and $g(d r u g)$ are weights of total drug added and drug entrapped in $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ , respectively.
## 2.3. Characterization of the samples
The crystalline structure of the samples was identified by powder Xray diffraction analysis (Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer) using CuKα radiation $(\lambda=1.5\dot{4}18\mathring\mathrm{A})$ . The X-ray scans were carried out for 2θ between 3 and $30^{\circ}$ at 0.02 increments. The X-ray diffraction data were recorded at ambient conditions in Bragg-Brentano geometry.
The surface area of the samples was determined by $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ adsorption/ desorption under liquid-nitrogen temperatures using a Nova Series 1200e (Quantachrome).
The $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra were obtained on a Bruker-AV-500 spectrometer in deuterated water.
The FTIR spectra of the samples were recorded using a VERTEX 80v FTIR spectrometer (Bruker). All samples were pressed into KBr pellets, and the spectra were scanned over a frequency range of $350{-}4000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .
Thermogravimetric analysis was performed using a Netzsch-TG 209 F1 Iris thermo-microbalance. The samples were heated from $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the rate of $10\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ .
## 2.4. Equilibrium solubility determination
Solubility of pure LEF, $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and γCD-MOF-2 as well as the physical mixtures of LEF with $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF was determined in buffer solutions $\mathrm{pH}1.2$ (0.06 M HCl) and $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ (phosphate buffer) by shake-flask method (Higuchi & Connors, 1965). The LEF content in physical mixtures was $20\mathrm{wt.}\%$ . Briefly, an excess amount of the sample in the appropriate buffer solution was placed in a capped tube. The tubes were subjected to shaking at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $500\mathrm{rpm}$ (Eppendorf ThermoMixer C) for $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ to reach the equilibrium. The supernatant was then separated from the bottom phase and centrifuged (Biofuge pico, Thermo Electron LED GmbH) at $6000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Concentration of drug in the solutions was determined spectrophotometrically (Shimadzu 1800) at the appropriate wavelength. The molar extinction coefficient (ε) of LEF was determined from the preliminary obtained calibration plots. Solutions used for ε determination were prepared by weight and, therefore, their concentration was expressed as molality (amount of moles of solute per kilogram of the solvent). Values of ε for LEF were $15,359\mathrm{kg/(molcm)}$ and $^{11,382\mathrm{kg/}}$ (mol cm) for buffer $\mathsf{p H}\ 1.2$ and 7.4, respectively, at wavelength $\lambda=260\mathrm{{nm}}$ . For TEF, the available in the literature (Sharma et al., 2016) $\varepsilon=10,500\mathrm{L}/(\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{cm})$ at $\lambda=291\mathrm{{nm}}$ was used. The absorption spectra of the samples after dissolution are given in SI (Fig. 1SI). The experiments were performed in triplicate with the accuracy of $2{-}4\%$ .
Buffer solutions were prepared on the basis of fresh bidistilled water. The pH of solutions was controlled by means of pH-meter Five Easy (Mettler Toledo).
## 2.5. Dissolution study
The dissolution experiments were carried out on USP dissolution tester (LanIndia DS 8000). The carefully weighted solid samples contained approximately equal amount $(\mathbf{\tilde{\omega}_{0.007}}\mathbf{g})$ of the active pharmaceutical ingredient. All the experiments were performed at temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and stirring speed of $50\mathrm{rpm}$ . The dissolution experiments were performed in buffer $\mathrm{pH}1.2$ (HCl) simulating the gastric juice and in phosphate buffer $\mathtt{p H}6.8$ modeling the intestine fluid. Aliquot of ${\boldsymbol{5}}\mathrm{mL}$ were periodically withdrawn from the dissolution medium and ${\mathsf{5}}{\mathrm{mL}}$ of the fresh buffer were added after each withdrawal. The amount of the released drug was determined spectrophotometrically (Shimadzu 1800, Japan). Each experiment was carried out in triplicate. The dependence of the mean cumulative percentage of the released drug on time was constructed and analyzed.
## 2.6. Permeability study
The permeability experiments were conducted in Franz diffusion cell (vertical type, PermeGear, Inc., PA, USA). A reverse dialysis was applied, namely, the bottom compartment of the cell $(5\mathrm{mL})$ was filled with the test solution, and the top compartment – with the fresh buffer (1 mL). Buffer $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ approximated to the blood plasma was used in order to simulate the physiological conditions. New specific artificial Permeapad™ barrier used herein as a membrane was proposed by Di Cagno, Bibi, and Bauer-Brandl (2015) for effective permeability evaluation. It has been checked that Permeapad™ barrier is compatible with surfactants, co-solvents and simulated intestinal fluids (Bibi, di Cagno, Holm, & Bauer-Brandl, 2015). The effective surface of the barrier was $0.64\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The constant temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was maintained in Franz cell, the donor solution was stirred vigorously. An aliquot of $0.5\mathrm{mL}$ was withdrawn from the top (receptor) compartment each $30\mathrm{min}$ , and replaced with an equal amount of fresh buffer $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ . The concentrations at each time point were analyzed spectrophotometrically (Shimadzu UV-1800). The plots of the amount of permeated drug over the surface area $(d Q/A)$ on the time $(t)$ were constructed, and the flux $(J)$ was calculated as slope of permeation plots by Eq. (3):
$$
J=\frac{d Q}{A\times d t}
$$
The apparent permeability coefficients $(P_{a p p})$ were calculated by normalizing the fluxes over the concentration of the substance in the donor compartment by Eq. (4):
$$
P_{a p p}={\frac{J}{C_{0}}}
$$
The value of $P_{a p p}$ was taken as an average of at least 3 repeated experiments. The sink conditions were maintained through the permeability experiment: the drug concentration in the acceptor chamber did not exceed $10\%$ of the drug concentration in the donor chamber at any time.
## 3. Results and discussion
The ${\tt\gamma}_{\mathrm{{VCD-MOF}}}$ was obtained by vapor diffusion method. The synthesis procedure is well described in literature (Smaldone et al., 2010). To confirm the structure of the synthesized $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ , a characterization of the particles by powder X-ray diffraction was carried out. The XRD patterns of the $\mathsf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}$ are shown in Fig. 2. As one can see, the experimental XRD pattern of the free $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ (Fig. 2b) is in a good agreement with the calculated one (Fig. 2a). This result demonstrates that $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ is crystalline, and diffraction peaks are consistent with those reported in the literature (Smaldone et al., 2010). It should be also noted that γCD-MOF crystallizes in the I432 space group which is typical for CD-MOFs prepared on the basis of alkali metal hydroxides
(Hartlieb et al., 2017).
The specific surface area and pore size of the vacuum-dried $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}.$ MOF samples were determined using low-temperature nitrogen adsorption/desorption method. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms are shown in Fig. 3. The shape of the isotherm allows to predict the type of the pores present in the adsorbent (Anovitz & Cole, 2015). According to the IUPAC classification, the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ isotherm that goes to the plateau (Fig. 3) corresponds to I-type characteristic for the microporous material. The surface area of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ calculated by BET method was equal to $618\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . This value falls in the range of the literature data. It has been demonstrated that $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF surface area varies from $495\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Liu et al., 2017) to $1220\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Smaldone et al., 2010) depending on the synthesis details. For comparison, $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ alone shows no uptake of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ (Forgan et al., 2012; Gassensmith et al., 2011). For $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF, the total pore volume was $0.31\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ .
The results of physicochemical investigation demonstrate that crystalline and porous $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF was successfully synthesized. On the next step of our work the loading of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ by LEF was performed by impregnation in saturated ethanol solution and by co-crystallization methods. The content of drug in γCD-MOF-1 and $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{CD}{\cdot}\mathbf{MOF}{-}2$ was determined by dissolution of the samples in phosphate buffer $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ followed by UV-spectroscopy analysis. The drug content in $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2 was found to be $14\mathrm{wt.}\%$ and 8 wt. $\%$ , respectively. The encapsulation efficiency of drug was $37\mathrm{wt.}\%$ and $21\mathrm{wt.}\%$ for $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}.$ MOF-1 and ${\tt\gamma}_{\mathrm{Y}}\mathrm{CD}{-}\mathrm{MOF}{-}2\$ , respectively.
It was interesting to note that the absorption spectrum of LEF was changed during the impregnation of drug in $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF. UV-spectra of supernatant were periodically recorded and transformation of LEF in TEF was detected (Fig. 2SI). LEF in ethanol and aqueous solutions has an absorption maximum at $260~\pm{}~1\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The UV-spectrum of TEF displays two maxima at $249\mathrm{nm}$ and $291\mathrm{nm}$ . As it is known from the literature (Sharma et al., 2016), the conversion of LEF to TEF is initiated in vitro by hydroxyl ions acting as catalysts. We suppose that γCD-MOF can be a source of hydroxyl ions, which induce the LEF transformation into TEF through the isoxazole ring opening. Kinetics of transformation process is depicted in Fig. 2SI (insert). As it was detected, the LEF transformation to TEF ends in $29\mathrm{h}$ in the presence of γCD-MOF in ethanol solution at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The presence of TEF in $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ was confirmed by $^{1}\mathrm{H}\ \mathrm{NMR}$ . The $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and $\upgamma{\bf C D-M O F-}2$ samples were dissolved in deuterated water and $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of the resulting solutions were recorded and compared with the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of LEF (Fig. 3SI). The $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of LEF in $\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}_{2}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ showed a singlet at $\delta2.61\mathrm{ppm}$ corresponding to $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ protons attached to the isoxazole ring; two doublets at 7.70 and $7.63\mathrm{ppm}$ for $-\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{C H}$ protons of phenyl ring system and singlet at $8.70\mathrm{ppm}$ for $-\mathbf{N}{=}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}-$ proton that is consistent with the literature data (Saini & Bansal, 2015). According to Saini and Bansal (2015), the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of TEF and LEF are similar and only one difference is that a signal due to $-\mathbf{N}{=}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}-$ proton is absent in case of TEF. The $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and ${\tt\gamma}_{\mathrm{Y}}\mathrm{CD}{-}\mathrm{MOF}{-}2$ dissolved in ${\bf D}_{2}0$ displayed peaks characteristic for TEF (Fig. 3SI) indicating that both γCD-MOFs contained TEF.
The loading of TEF in $\mathsf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathsf{C D-M O F}$ was also revealed using FTIR spectroscopy. The FTIR spectra of all the tested samples are given in Fig. 4. The FTIR spectrum of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ (Fig. 4) shows a broad absorption peak in the region of $3030{-}3670\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ reflecting the OH group stretching vibrations in the glucose units, peak at $2928\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the stretching vibrations of $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ bonds from the $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ and CH groups, the peaks at 1642, 1421 and $1159\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to OH group bending vibrations, as well as at $1027.0\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $859\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ related to $_{\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{O}}$ and $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ stretching vibrations. The presence of similar peaks in $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ spectrum (Fig. 4) shows the keeping of the skeleton structure of the parent $\upgamma$ -CD during the MOF formation.
The FTIR spectrum of LEF shows characteristic peaks at $3358\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (NH-group of amide fragment), $3070\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (aromatic $\mathrm{C-H})$ , $1692\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\scriptstyle\mathtt{.-C=0}$ stretching of secondary amides), $1604\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (skeletal bands). All these peaks were observed in FTIR spectra of the physical mixtures of LEF with $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and γCD-MOF (Fig. 4) indicating any interactions between the components.
According to the literature data (Saini & Bansal, 2015), the main difference between FTIR spectra of LEF and TEF is an absence of the signal attributed to $-c{\equiv}\mathbf{N}$ group in spectrum of LEF and its availability in TEF spectrum at $2220\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . As one can see from Fig. 4, peaks at $2195\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are present in FTIR spectra of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2 indicating the loading of TEF into the framework of the carrier.
As one can see, impregnation method yields higher drug loading $(14\mathrm{wt.\%})$ in comparison with co-crystallization $(8\mathrm{wt.\%})$ . It was in accordance with the measured specific surface area of the loaded samples (Fig. 3). In particular, $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 has a surface area $46\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , whereas the $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2 has a surface area of $220\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ corresponding to the incomplete loading. Thus, the location of drug in the porous structure of $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF results in dramatic decrease in the surface area.
The powder X-ray diffraction analysis revealed that inclusion of drug into $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ has no impact on its crystallinity and crystal structure (Fig. 2c,d). As follows from Fig. 2, no evidence of phase transformation or disruption of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ was observed during the loading process. In addition, the reflections corresponding to the pure LEF (Fig. 1e) or TEF (characteristic reflections at 8.2, 12.4, 14.9, 16.3, 18.7, 21.1, 23.5, 25.3, 26.6, 27.9, 28.7, $33.2^{\circ}$ (Siafaka et al., 2016) were absent in the X-ray diffraction patterns of $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2, suggesting that the drug was incorporated in an amorphous form in the γCD-MOF.
Thermal stability of empty and loaded $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOFs was examined. The results of TGA are given in Fig. 5. The TG curve of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ (Fig. 5) shows that the weight loss of the sample takes place in the temperature interval from $47^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $103^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ This process corresponds to the removal of the residual solvent. The thermal degradation of $\gamma{\mathrm{CD}}.$ - MOF starts at $243^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . This is consistent with the literature data (Abuçafy et al., 2018; Forgan et al., 2012) reporting that $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ is stable till $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Melting of TEF followed by decomposition begins at $167^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Siafaka et al., 2016). The TGA curves obtained for $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{CD-MOF-1}$ and γCD-MOF-2 (Fig. 5) do not display the TEF mass loss in the temperature range from $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $210^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ indicating the thermal stabilization of drug due to the location in the porous structure of $\mathsf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{Y C D-M O F}}$ .
Solubility in physiologically relevant media is a key parameter for any drug compound since it determines its bioavailability. The equilibrium solubility of pure LEF as well as $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{CD-MOF-}2$ was measured in buffer solutions at physiological $\mathrm{pH}1.2$ and $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ and temperature of $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Solubility of all the investigated samples is presented in Fig. 6. As it concerns TEF, to the best of our knowledge it is insoluble or only slightly soluble in water and various organic solvents (Bae & Park, 2015). According to biopharmaceutical classification system TEF belongs to Class 2 compounds (Asheesh & Parul, 2016).
As it is evident from Fig. 6, solubility of pure LEF practically is not changed with the pH variation. This is due to predominance of the neutral form of LEF molecule in both buffers under study. The experimentally obtained $\mathrm{pK_{a}}=9.3$ of LEF has been reported in the literature (Yilmaz & Demiralay, 2015). The predicted $\mathsf{p K}_{\mathrm{a}}$ value was equal to 10.4. Physical mixtures of LEF with $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathrm{{Y}}}\mathrm{{CD-MOF}}$ display a slight increase in solubility. When dissolving physical mixtures $\operatorname{LEF}/\gamma$ - CD and LEF/ $\ensuremath{\gamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF, $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ goes in solution and dissolves. Presence of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ in solution initiates the LEF dissolution and observed enhancement of LEF solubility can be due to inclusion complex formation. However, the binding affinity of LEF to $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ is weak and, as a consequence, solubility increase is negligible (Fig. 6). It has been demonstrated in our recent publication (Terekhova et al., 2016) that solubilizing action of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ towards the LEF was insignificant. Herein, complex formation of LEF with $\upgamma$ -CD in aqueous solution was studied by means of $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR. Stoichiometry of the complexes was obtained using Job’s method (Job, 1928) (Fig. 4SI) and stability constant of the inclusion complexes was calculated from the concentration dependences of Δδ (Fig. 5SI) using nonlinear least squares curve-fitting procedure. Stability constant of $\operatorname{LEF}/\gamma$ -CD complexes (1:1) was found to be $98\mathrm{kg/}$ mol. As one can see, this value is not high and points out weak complexation and solubilization.
In comparison with pure LEF as well as its physical mixtures with $\gamma\cdot$ - CD and $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ , the γCD-MOF-1 and $\mathrm{\vCD-MOF{-}}2$ demonstrate the decreased solubility in buffer $\mathrm{pH}1.2$ (Fig. 6). This can be caused by very low solubility of TEF in the acidic medium. On the contrary, the solubility $\mathrm{\gamma{\ttCD-MOF-1}}$ and $\mathrm{\gamma}_{\mathrm{{Y}}}\mathrm{{CD-MOF-}}2$ dramatically increases in buffer pH 7.4. In particular, 80-fold and 30-fold solubility increase was obtained for $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and $\upgamma{\mathrm{CD}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MOF}}{-2}$ , respectively. Thus, TEF in porous $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ - MOF structure displays the improved solubility in buffer $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ . Considerable difference in the behavior of the loaded $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOFs in buffers with $\mathrm{pH}1.2$ and $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ (Fig. 6) can be explained by two factors. First of them is different ionization state of TEF in buffers $\mathrm{pH}1.2$ and 7.4. This follows from $\mathrm{pK_{a}}=5.2$ reported in the literature (Xi et al., 2013). The second reason is possible high binding affinity of TEF to $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ , which is present in the solution after dissolution of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2.
Considerable difference in the solubility of $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and $\gamma\mathrm{CD}.$ MOF-2 was revealed (Fig. 6). It is well known that $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ is soluble in aqueous solution. It means that dissolving $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF crystals in water results in dissociation of framework (Moussa et al., 2016). Thus, the loaded γCD-MOFs get collapsed to release the drug. As follows from the results of powder X-ray diffraction analysis (Fig. 2), the drug encapsulated in $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ is in the amorphous state. Amorphous materials have high energy state which gives rise to their improved apparent solubility. More probably, that loading of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ by different methods results in the difference in the amorphous character of the encapsulated TEF. As consequence, we see the different apparent solubility of $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2 (Fig. 6). Additionally, potassium cations, which appear in solution when $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ is dissolved, can affect the drug solubility, showing the salting out effect. In case of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ - MOF-2, $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions are formed more compared with γCD-MOF-1 and their influence of drug solubility is more pronounced.
Besides the solubility, the dissolution rate is also of a great importance for oral dosage forms. In the present study, the dissolution of initial LEF as well as $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{CD}{\cdot}\mathbf{MOF}{-}2$ was investigated. Two dissolution media were tried in these experiments – $\mathrm{pH}1.2$ modeling the gastric juice and $\mathtt{p H}6.8$ simulating the intestinal fluid. It was revealed that at $\mathrm{pH}1.2$ the dissolution of all the samples under consideration was extremely slow process (data not shown). Only $1{-}2\%$ dissolved during $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ . It was consistent with the literature data showing that release profile of the pure TEF is obvious that even after $132\mathrm{h}$ in SBF medium $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ its dissolution is less than $1\%$ (Siafaka et al., 2016).
The dissolution profiles of pure LEF and prepared formulations at $\mathtt{p H}6.8$ are shown in Fig. 7. As one can see from Fig. 7, $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2 display a significant enhancement of the dissolution rate. Only $20\%$ of pure LEF dissolved in $400\mathrm{min}$ , while $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ - MOF-2 completely dissolved in $100\mathrm{{min}}$ and $60\mathrm{min}$ , respectively. Faster dissolution of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2 can be explained by the availability of empty pores in this material (Fig. 3). In this case, the solvent fills the pores, which contributes to the destruction of the matrix and faster drug release.
It was interesting to reveal the driving forces of the dissolution process. To this end, kinetic models are usually used to characterize the dissolution (Costa & Lobo, 2001; Singhvi & Singh, 2011). Zero-order, first-order, Hixson-Crowell cube root law and Korsemeyer–Peppas models were tried herein for determination of release mechanisms (Table 1). It was found that the dissolution of pure LEF in buffer $\mathrm{pH}6.8$ follows zero-order model. The mathematical treatment of the dissolution profiles of $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and $\mathrm{\gamma}_{\mathrm{Y}}\mathrm{CD-MOF-}2$ by Korsemeyer–Peppas model gave the best results (Table 1). The Korsemeyer–Peppas model allows to determine the release mechanism according to the following equation:
$$
M_{t}/M_{\infty}=k t^{n}
$$
where $M_{t}$ is the amount of the drug released at time t, $M_{\infty}$ is the amount of drug released over a very long time that corresponds in principle to the initial loading, $k$ is the kinetic constant and $n$ is the release exponent (Peppas, 1985). The exponent $n$ describing the mechanism of drug release was determined for the segment of the release curve where $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{t}}/\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $\mathbf{M}_{\infty}~<~0.6$ . The regression parameters obtained for the release kinetics from the in vitro dissolution data (Fig. 7) are given in Table 1. As it has been reported (Peppas, 1985; Sinclair & Peppas, 1984), two competing release mechanisms limit the dissolution phenomenon: a Fickian diffusional release and a Case-II relaxational release determined by restructuration of polymeric chains and supramolecular structures. From the fitting of Korsemeyer–Peppas model it can be concluded that anomalous transport involving both diffusion-controlled and erosioncontrolled processes was observed for both MOFs $(0.5\mathrm{~<~n~<~}1)$ ) (Peppas, 1985; Sinclair & Peppas, 1984).
Permeability is also very important property of drugs determining their bioavailability. From this point of view, the permeability of pure LEF, its physical mixture with $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ as well as the obtained $\mathrm{\gamma_{\mathrm{\ell}}C D-M O F-1}$ and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-2 was examined. The experimental data of the permeability are presented in Figs. 8 and 9 as the kinetic dependences of the cumulative amount of the compound permeated through the Permeapad barrier and permeability coefficients, respectively.
Comparative analysis of the fluxes $J$ (Fig. 8) and permeability coefficients $P_{a p p}$ (Fig. 9) shows that $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ being in the physical mixture with LEF slightly decreases $P_{a p p}$ due to the weak complex formation of drug with cyclodextrin. The $\upgamma\mathrm{CD}$ -MOF-1 and $\mathsf{\gamma}_{\mathsf{Y}}\mathbf{CD-MOF-}2$ have higher $J$ and $P_{a p p}$ values in comparison with pure LEF. Based on the obtained results, it can be proposed that TEF released from $\mathrm{\gamma{cm-MOF-1}}$ and $\gamma\mathrm{CD}.$ MOF-2 has improved permeability compared with the initial LEF. These results indicate a significant superiority of the $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ compared to other methods commonly applied to increase the solubility of lipophilic compounds (for example, solid dispersions with cyclodextrins and polymers), in the case of which the permeability is often significantly reduced by deceasing the proportion of free molecules capable of pass through the membrane.
## 4. Conclusions
This work shows that metal-organic framework based on $\gamma$ -cyclodextrin and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions serves as an effective carrier of leflunomide – immunosuppressive disease-modifying antirheumatic drug. It was revealed that in the presence of $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ leflunomide converts to its active open-ring malononitrile metabolite – teriflunomide, which was also approved and licensed in 2012 for the treatment of multiple sclerosis. It was demonstrated that leflunomide can be loaded into $\gamma{\mathrm{CD}}.$ - MOF by impregnation and co-crystallization methods, the former is more effective and gives the higher drug content. The solubility, dissolution and permeation behavior of the loaded $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ in simulated physiological conditions was investigated for considering the possibility of pharmaceutical application. It was observed that teriflunomide released from $\scriptstyle\gamma\mathbf{CD-MOF}$ displays improved solubility and permeability. The revealed features in the behaviour of leflunomide encapsulated into γCD-MOF allow to propose the novel strategy for delivery of this drug.
